---- DATA STRUCTURES ----

>> C1: Copy here the declaration of each new or changed `struct' or
>> `struct' member, global or static variable, `typedef', or
>> enumeration.  Identify the purpose of each in 25 words or less.

#define BUFFER_CACHE_ENTRY_NB 64 : number of buffer cache entry.
#define SECTOR_SIZE 512 : size of each sector.

struct buffer_head { : buffer cache head structure
    bool dirty; : set to 1 if cache is written.
    bool is_used; : set to 1 if cache is currently used.
    block_sector_t sector; : points the sector number of the disk.
    bool clock_bit; : clock bit for clock algorithm to select a victim.
    struct lock buffer_lock; : lock for each cache entry.
    void *data; : contains data which will be in the disk.
};

// Global variables
void *p_buffer_cache; : Points the memory region of the buffer caches
struct buffer_head head_buffer[BUFFER_CACHE_ENTRY_NB]; : Array of buffer head
int clock_hand; : Variable for clock algorithm; hold current clock entry.
struct lock clock_hand_lock; : Lock for clock hand

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> C2: Describe how your cache replacement algorithm chooses a cache
>> block to evict.

My replacement algorithm is clock algorithm.
First, check if there is unused entry. If there is, select it as a victim.
If not, search buffer cache entry in clockwise direction starting from clock_hand.
If there's a entry whose clock_bit is false, write data to the disk if it is dirty,
and then select it as a victim. If not, flip the clock_bit to false, keep searching.

>> C3: Describe your implementation of write-behind.

If cache hit, write data on the buffer cache. If cache miss,
select victim and write data on the disk if the cache is dirty, and then,
write data to the newly selected cache.
When file system shuts down, write all the dirty cache on the disk.

>> C4: Describe your implementation of read-ahead.

If cache hit, read data from the buffer cache. If cache miss,
select victim and write data on the disk if the cache is dirty, and then,
read data from the newly selected cache.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> C5: When one process is actively reading or writing data in a
>> buffer cache block, how are other processes prevented from evicting
>> that block?

Each cache block has its own lock, and I set the lock when the program
reads or changes the any members of the buffer cache's buffer head.
Thus, each buffer cache could be accessed by only one thread.

>> C6: During the eviction of a block from the cache, how are other
>> processes prevented from attempting to access the block?

I also set the clock_hand_lock and the lock of each buffer cache entry
thus other process prevented from accessing the block during the eviction.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C7: Describe a file workload likely to benefit from buffer caching,
>> and workloads likely to benefit from read-ahead and write-behind.

By using buffer cache, read/write workload is likely to be reduced, since
it can read/write the data from the memory which is much faster than
read/write the data from the disk. If cache hits, read-ahead reduce workload
to read data from the memory so that it do not have to access the disk. Also,
write-behind reduce workload to write data on the memory and do not have to
write on the disk every time.

